"},{"line_number":312,"content":"1. The write copies user bytes into page-cache pages of the file's address space, allocating pages and inode blocks as the file grows."},{"line_number":313,"content":"2. Those pages are marked dirty."},{"line_number":314,"content":"3. The process returns, unless durability was requested."},{"line_number":315,"content":"4. Writeback selects dirty pages, maps each file offset through the inode or extent tree to a device sector, and builds a bio."},{"line_number":316,"content":"5. The block layer queues the bio on the multi-queue path and the driver programs DMA."},{"line_number":317,"content":"6. Completion marks the pages clean, or reports an error to anyone waiting on `fsync`."},{"line_number":516,"content":"> [!question]- A 5 MiB file uses 4 KiB blocks and a classic inode with 4-byte pointers. How many indirect blocks does it allocate, and how many block reads fetch the last byte if the inode is cached? (concept: R02-02)"},{"line_number":522,"content":"> [!question]- A 4096-byte TCP payload goes out on a 1500-byte MTU with no options, no VLAN, and TSO off. How many segments, how many on-wire frame bytes including FCS, and how many CPU payload copies does a normal send perform? (concepts: R02-04, R02-05)"}]}]},"locations":[]}
{"type":"thought","data":"The official Linux kernel"}
{"type":"thought","data":" documentation indicates"}
{"type":"thought","data":" the EEVDF transition"}
{"type":"thought","data":" began in version 6."}
{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nEEVDF was introduced"}
{"type":"thought","data":" as an option in 2024,"}
{"type":"thought","data":" then became unconditional"}
{"type":"thought","data":" in Linux 6.6. The kernel"}
{"type":"thought","data":" documentation URL should"}
{"type":"thought","data":" be verified and cited."}
{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nRHEL 10 documentation"}
{"type":"thought","data":" explicitly states CFS"}
{"type":"thought","data":" is replaced by EEVDF."}
{"type":"thought","data":" The kernel.org phrasing"}
{"type":"thought","data":" about a 2024 option"}
{"type":"thought","data":" is imprecise. The revised"}
{"type":"thought","data":" claim will state that"}
{"type":"thought","data":" Linux's fair class "}
{"type":"thought","data":"moved from CFS to EEVDF"}
{"type":"thought","data":" starting in version"}
{"type":"thought","data":" 6.6, with current kernels"}
{"type":"thought","data":" scheduling that class"}
{"type":"thought","data":" inside NUMA sched domains."}
{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nThe unconditional "}
{"type":"thought","data":"EEVDF commit was merged"}
{"type":"thought","data":" in the Linux 6.6 window."}
{"type":"thought","data":" The kernel documentation"}
{"type":"thought","data":" URL will be verified"}
{"type":"thought","data":" and added to further"}
{"type":"thought","data":" reading if accessible."}
{"type":"thought","data":" NUMA balancing language"}
{"type":"thought","data":" and privileged ports"}
{"type":"thought","data":" will be tightened. "}
{"type":"thought","data":"A sentence about memory"}
{"type":"thought","data":" hierarchy near a disk"}
{"type":"thought","data":" was identified as awkward."}
{"type":"thought","data":"\n\nCXL-attached memory"}
{"type":"thought","data":" is farther than local"}
{"type":"thought","data":" DRAM and well below"}
{"type":"thought","data":" disk latency. NUMA "}
{"type":"thought","data":"balancing uses hinting"}
{"type":"thought","data":" page faults rather than unmaps. The qspinlock replacement claim needs verification."}
