{"type":"tool_call","toolCallId":"call-908da7ae-8018-45f6-b447-aa182fc3f2da-40","title":"search_replace","kind":"edit","status":"pending","toolName":"search_replace","rawInput":{"file_path":"/Users/shreejitverma/.treehouse/SDE-Interview-Prep-3215ec/3/SDE-Interview-Prep/01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-0-Refresher/R02-Architecture-Storage-and-Networking.md","old_string":"Linux still first-touches anonymous pages, and automatic NUMA balancing can unmap and migrate a page that is being faulted from the wrong node.\nThe scheduler that decides where the faulting thread runs is the fair class, which has used EEVDF since Linux 6.6 and still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).","new_string":"Linux still first-touches anonymous pages.\nAutomatic NUMA balancing, controlled by `kernel.numa_balancing`, periodically unmaps sampled pages and, on the following fault, may migrate the page to the node that touched it.\nThe scheduler that decides where that thread runs is the fair class.\nThat class has been moving from CFS to EEVDF since Linux 6.6, and it still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains)."},"content":[],"locations":[]}
