{"type":"tool_call_update","toolCallId":"call-908da7ae-8018-45f6-b447-aa182fc3f2da-40","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/Users/shreejitverma/.treehouse/SDE-Interview-Prep-3215ec/3/SDE-Interview-Prep/01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-0-Refresher/R02-Architecture-Storage-and-Networking.md","oldText":"Linux still first-touches anonymous pages, and automatic NUMA balancing can unmap and migrate a page that is being faulted from the wrong node.\nThe scheduler that decides where the faulting thread runs is the fair class, which has used EEVDF since Linux 6.6 and still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).","newText":"Linux still first-touches anonymous pages.\nAutomatic NUMA balancing, controlled by `kernel.numa_balancing`, periodically unmaps sampled pages and, on the following fault, may migrate the page to the node that touched it.\nThe scheduler that decides where that thread runs is the fair class.\nThat class has been moving from CFS to EEVDF since Linux 6.6, and it still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).","_meta":{"details":[{"old_string":"Linux still first-touches anonymous pages, and automatic NUMA balancing can unmap and migrate a page that is being faulted from the wrong node.\nThe scheduler that decides where the faulting thread runs is the fair class, which has used EEVDF since Linux 6.6 and still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).","old_line":444,"new_string":"Linux still first-touches anonymous pages.\nAutomatic NUMA balancing, controlled by `kernel.numa_balancing`, periodically unmaps sampled pages and, on the following fault, may migrate the page to the node that touched it.\nThe scheduler that decides where that thread runs is the fair class.\nThat class has been moving from CFS to EEVDF since Linux 6.6, and it still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).","new_line":444,"context_before":"## Modern descendants\n\nCurrent multi-socket servers are ccNUMA: every CPU can run the kernel, caches are coherent, and DRAM latency depends on the node.\n","context_after":"[qspinlock](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04b-Synchronization.md#modern-descendants-linux-qspinlock) is the kernel lock that replaced a plain ticket lock on this hardware, because a single shared ticket line is a remote atomic on every spinner's node.\nCXL memory devices, enumerated by the [kernel CXL subsystem](https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/cxl/index.html), add another distance: the memory can be onlined as a node that is farther than local DRAM and closer, usually, than a disk.\nPlacement policies then have more than two answers.\n","line_prefix":""}]}}],"rawOutput":{"type":"SearchReplace","EditsApplied":{"old_string":"Linux still first-touches anonymous pages, and automatic NUMA balancing can unmap and migrate a page that is being faulted from the wrong node.\nThe scheduler that decides where the faulting thread runs is the fair class, which has used EEVDF since Linux 6.6 and still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).","new_string":"Linux still first-touches anonymous pages.\nAutomatic NUMA balancing, controlled by `kernel.numa_balancing`, periodically unmaps sampled pages and, on the following fault, may migrate the page to the node that touched it.\nThe scheduler that decides where that thread runs is the fair class.\nThat class has been moving from CFS to EEVDF since Linux 6.6, and it still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).","tool_output_for_prompt":"The file /Users/shreejitverma/.treehouse/SDE-Interview-Prep-3215ec/3/SDE-Interview-Prep/01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-0-Refresher/R02-Architecture-Storage-and-Networking.md has been updated successfully.","tool_output_for_prompt_concise":"The file /Users/shreejitverma/.treehouse/SDE-Interview-Prep-3215ec/3/SDE-Interview-Prep/01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-0-Refresher/R02-Architecture-Storage-and-Networking.md has been updated.","absolute_path":"/Users/shreejitverma/.treehouse/SDE-Interview-Prep-3215ec/3/SDE-Interview-Prep/01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-0-Refresher/R02-Architecture-Storage-and-Networking.md","edits":{"details":[{"old_string":"Linux still first-touches anonymous pages, and automatic NUMA balancing can unmap and migrate a page that is being faulted from the wrong node.\nThe scheduler that decides where the faulting thread runs is the fair class, which has used EEVDF since Linux 6.6 and still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).","old_line":444,"new_string":"Linux still first-touches anonymous pages.\nAutomatic NUMA balancing, controlled by `kernel.numa_balancing`, periodically unmaps sampled pages and, on the following fault, may migrate the page to the node that touched it.\nThe scheduler that decides where that thread runs is the fair class.\nThat class has been moving from CFS to EEVDF since Linux 6.6, and it still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).","new_line":444,"context_before":"## Modern descendants\n\nCurrent multi-socket servers are ccNUMA: every CPU can run the kernel, caches are coherent, and DRAM latency depends on the node.\n","context_after":"[qspinlock](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04b-Synchronization.md#modern-descendants-linux-qspinlock) is the kernel lock that replaced a plain ticket lock on this hardware, because a single shared ticket line is a remote atomic on every spinner's node.\nCXL memory devices, enumerated by the [kernel CXL subsystem](https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/cxl/index.html), add another distance: the memory can be onlined as a node that is farther than local DRAM and closer, usually, than a disk.\nPlacement policies then have more than two answers.\n","line_prefix":""}]}}},"locations":[]}
