{"type":"tool_call_update","toolCallId":"call-85efa187-d68b-4972-9107-0c23882ed57d-48","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"text","text":"446→Current multi-socket servers are ccNUMA: every CPU can run the kernel, caches are coherent, and DRAM latency depends on the node.\nLinux still first-touches anonymous pages.\nAutomatic NUMA balancing, controlled by `kernel.numa_balancing`, periodically unmaps sampled pages and, on the following fault, may migrate the page to the node that touched it.\nThe scheduler that decides where that thread runs is the fair class.\n450→That class has been moving from CFS to EEVDF since Linux 6.6, and it still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).\n[qspinlock](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04b-Synchronization.md#modern-descendants-linux-qspinlock) is the kernel lock that replaced a plain ticket lock on this hardware, because a single shared ticket line is a remote atomic on every spinner's node.\nCXL memory devices, enumerated by the [kernel CXL subsystem](https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/cxl/index.html), add another distance: the memory can be onlined as a node that is farther than local DRAM and closer, usually, than a disk.\nPlacement policies then have more than two answers."}}],"rawOutput":{"type":"ReadFile","FileContent":{"content":"446→Current multi-socket servers are ccNUMA: every CPU can run the kernel, caches are coherent, and DRAM latency depends on the node.\nLinux still first-touches anonymous pages.\nAutomatic NUMA balancing, controlled by `kernel.numa_balancing`, periodically unmaps sampled pages and, on the following fault, may migrate the page to the node that touched it.\nThe scheduler that decides where that thread runs is the fair class.\n450→That class has been moving from CFS to EEVDF since Linux 6.6, and it still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).\n[qspinlock](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04b-Synchronization.md#modern-descendants-linux-qspinlock) is the kernel lock that replaced a plain ticket lock on this hardware, because a single shared ticket line is a remote atomic on every spinner's node.\nCXL memory devices, enumerated by the [kernel CXL subsystem](https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/cxl/index.html), add another distance: the memory can be onlined as a node that is farther than local DRAM and closer, usually, than a disk.\nPlacement policies then have more than two answers.","content_concise":"446→Current multi-socket servers are ccNUMA: every CPU can run the kernel, caches are coherent, and DRAM latency depends on the node.\nLinux still first-touches anonymous pages.\nAutomatic NUMA balancing, controlled by `kernel.numa_balancing`, periodically unmaps sampled pages and, on the following fault, may migrate the page to the node that touched it.\nThe scheduler that decides where that thread runs is the fair class.\n450→That class has been moving from CFS to EEVDF since Linux 6.6, and it still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).\n[qspinlock](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04b-Synchronization.md#modern-descendants-linux-qspinlock) is the kernel lock that replaced a plain ticket lock on this hardware, because a single shared ticket line is a remote atomic on every spinner's node.\nCXL memory devices, enumerated by the [kernel CXL subsystem](https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/cxl/index.html), add another distance: the memory can be onlined as a node that is farther than local DRAM and closer, usually, than a disk.\nPlacement policies then have more than two answers.","absolute_path":"/Users/shreejitverma/.treehouse/SDE-Interview-Prep-3215ec/3/SDE-Interview-Prep/01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-0-Refresher/R02-Architecture-Storage-and-Networking.md","offset":446,"limit":8,"raw_output":"Current multi-socket servers are ccNUMA: every CPU can run the kernel, caches are coherent, and DRAM latency depends on the node.\nLinux still first-touches anonymous pages.\nAutomatic NUMA balancing, controlled by `kernel.numa_balancing`, periodically unmaps sampled pages and, on the following fault, may migrate the page to the node that touched it.\nThe scheduler that decides where that thread runs is the fair class.\nThat class has been moving from CFS to EEVDF since Linux 6.6, and it still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).\n[qspinlock](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04b-Synchronization.md#modern-descendants-linux-qspinlock) is the kernel lock that replaced a plain ticket lock on this hardware, because a single shared ticket line is a remote atomic on every spinner's node.\nCXL memory devices, enumerated by the [kernel CXL subsystem](https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/cxl/index.html), add another distance: the memory can be onlined as a node that is farther than local DRAM and closer, usually, than a disk.\nPlacement policies then have more than two answers.\n","total_lines":565}},"locations":[]}
