{"type":"tool_call_update","toolCallId":"call-a1aad098-6894-4bfa-80d0-45abd085ce72-51","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/Users/shreejitverma/.treehouse/SDE-Interview-Prep-3215ec/3/SDE-Interview-Prep/01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-0-Refresher/R02-Architecture-Storage-and-Networking.md","oldText":"That class has been moving from CFS to EEVDF since Linux 6.6, and it still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).\n[qspinlock](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04b-Synchronization.md#modern-descendants-linux-qspinlock) is the kernel lock that replaced a plain ticket lock on this hardware, because a single shared ticket line is a remote atomic on every spinner's node.\nCXL memory devices, enumerated by the [kernel CXL subsystem](https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/cxl/index.html), add another distance: the memory can be onlined as a node that is farther than local DRAM and closer, usually, than a disk.","newText":"On current kernels that class is EEVDF, the fair-class scheduler that replaced CFS starting in Linux 6.6, and it still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).\n[qspinlock](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04b-Synchronization.md#modern-descendants-linux-qspinlock) is the kernel lock that replaced a ticket lock on large machines, because the ticket counter is one cache line that remote spinners bounce across the interconnect.\nCXL memory devices, enumerated by the [kernel CXL subsystem](https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/cxl/index.html), can be onlined as an extra NUMA node with its own distance from the CPUs.","_meta":{"details":[{"old_string":"That class has been moving from CFS to EEVDF since Linux 6.6, and it still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).\n[qspinlock](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04b-Synchronization.md#modern-descendants-linux-qspinlock) is the kernel lock that replaced a plain ticket lock on this hardware, because a single shared ticket line is a remote atomic on every spinner's node.\nCXL memory devices, enumerated by the [kernel CXL subsystem](https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/cxl/index.html), add another distance: the memory can be onlined as a node that is farther than local DRAM and closer, usually, than a disk.","old_line":450,"new_string":"On current kernels that class is EEVDF, the fair-class scheduler that replaced CFS starting in Linux 6.6, and it still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).\n[qspinlock](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04b-Synchronization.md#modern-descendants-linux-qspinlock) is the kernel lock that replaced a ticket lock on large machines, because the ticket counter is one cache line that remote spinners bounce across the interconnect.\nCXL memory devices, enumerated by the [kernel CXL subsystem](https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/cxl/index.html), can be onlined as an extra NUMA node with its own distance from the CPUs.","new_line":450,"context_before":"Linux still first-touches anonymous pages.\nAutomatic NUMA balancing, controlled by `kernel.numa_balancing`, periodically unmaps sampled pages and, on the following fault, may migrate the page to the node that touched it.\nThe scheduler that decides where that thread runs is the fair class.\n","context_after":"Placement policies then have more than two answers.\n\nNVMe presents many queue pairs, and [blk-mq](https://docs.kernel.org/block/blk-mq.html) is the block layer that drives them.\n","line_prefix":""}]}}],"rawOutput":{"type":"SearchReplace","EditsApplied":{"old_string":"That class has been moving from CFS to EEVDF since Linux 6.6, and it still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).\n[qspinlock](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04b-Synchronization.md#modern-descendants-linux-qspinlock) is the kernel lock that replaced a plain ticket lock on this hardware, because a single shared ticket line is a remote atomic on every spinner's node.\nCXL memory devices, enumerated by the [kernel CXL subsystem](https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/cxl/index.html), add another distance: the memory can be onlined as a node that is farther than local DRAM and closer, usually, than a disk.","new_string":"On current kernels that class is EEVDF, the fair-class scheduler that replaced CFS starting in Linux 6.6, and it still schedules inside [NUMA sched domains](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04e-Scheduling.md#modern-descendants-linux-cfs-and-eevdf-sched-domains).\n[qspinlock](../Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04b-Synchronization.md#modern-descendants-linux-qspinlock) is the kernel lock that replaced a ticket lock on large machines, because the ticket counter is one cache line that remote spinners bounce across the interconnect.\nCXL memory devices, enumerated by the [kernel CXL subsystem](https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/cxl/index.html), can be onlined as an extra NUMA node with its own distance from the CPUs.","tool_output_for_prompt":"The file /Users/shreejitverma/.treehouse/SDE-Interview-Prep-3215ec/3/SDE-Interview-Prep/01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-0-Refresher/R02-Architecture-Storage-and-Networking.md has been updated successfully.","tool_output_for_prompt_concise":"The file /Users/shreejitverma/.treehouse/SDE-Interview-Prep-3215ec/3/SDE-Interview-Prep/01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-0-Refresher/R02-Architecture-Storage-and-Networking.md has been updated.","absolute_path":"/Users/shreejitverma/.treehouse/SDE-Interview-Prep-3215ec/3/SDE-Interview-Prep/01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-0-Refresher/R02-Architecture-Storage-and-Networking.md","edits":{"details":[{"old_string"